\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Validation against the Memmingen measurements. \textbf  {Model} names the formulation each metric is evaluated against: the shipped KPIs are the \emph  {MILP baseline}, which uses a nominal constant return temperature and does not propagate the supply temperature. The cost conclusions depend on the annual delivered energy (hence network loss) and the heat-pump performance, both of which pass; the calibrated return temperature also matches within its ex-ante 2.5\,°C gate (bias only $-0.3$\,°C). The \emph  {supply}-temperature-propagation metrics (far-end, trunk drop, corridor) are \textbf  {n/a} for the MILP: their gates are defined for the non-linear (NLP) formulation only (\texttt  {VALIDATION\_METHODOLOGY.md}), and the MILP injects the source temperature as a boundary condition without propagating it -- so a zero simulated drop is by construction, not a fit failure. The physical temperature field is reproduced by the non-linear forward evaluator and anchored to the annual loss; point/corridor temperatures are additionally not validatable at this metering resolution (sensors downstream of mixing valves). Bias is signed mean error; scatter is RMSE.}}{18}{table.caption.23}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:validation}{{5}{18}{Validation against the Memmingen measurements. \textbf {Model} names the formulation each metric is evaluated against: the shipped KPIs are the \emph {MILP baseline}, which uses a nominal constant return temperature and does not propagate the supply temperature. The cost conclusions depend on the annual delivered energy (hence network loss) and the heat-pump performance, both of which pass; the calibrated return temperature also matches within its ex-ante 2.5\,°C gate (bias only $-0.3$\,°C). The \emph {supply}-temperature-propagation metrics (far-end, trunk drop, corridor) are \textbf {n/a} for the MILP: their gates are defined for the non-linear (NLP) formulation only (\texttt {VALIDATION\_METHODOLOGY.md}), and the MILP injects the source temperature as a boundary condition without propagating it -- so a zero simulated drop is by construction, not a fit failure. The physical temperature field is reproduced by the non-linear forward evaluator and anchored to the annual loss; point/corridor temperatures are additionally not validatable at this metering resolution (sensors downstream of mixing valves). Bias is signed mean error; scatter is RMSE}{table.caption.23}{}}
