\newlabel{fig:decomp}{{6}{22}{Exact decomposition of the copperplate-to-baseline cost gap into a loss main effect, a spatial-topology main effect and their interaction, for Memmingen (left) and as a distribution across the 135 synthetic networks (right). The additive identity closes to machine precision, so the three terms are measured rather than fitted}{figure.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Estimation bias versus decision regret (\% of the baseline $\ensuremath  {\mathrm  {L1}}$ operating cost, forward evaluator), with the \emph  {decision} each model drove. Regret is shown for three shortfall-pricing schemes -- top-up at the marginal unit, the peak unit, and unmet demand penalised at the dump price. \emph  {Viol.} is physical-feasibility violations (velocity, differential pressure, unmet). The last two columns are the resulting dispatch: heat-pump energy share and running hours. The copperplate, blind to network loss, under-provisions the heat pump ($77.9$\,\%, 2063\,h) relative to the loss-aware node-resolved levels ($87.8$\,\%, $\sim $2310\,h), and pays the shortfall at the margin in winter hours it never planned for -- which is what the $+46$\,\% regret \emph  {is}, physically.}}{22}{table.caption.25}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:regret}{{7}{22}{Estimation bias versus decision regret (\% of the baseline $\Lone $ operating cost, forward evaluator), with the \emph {decision} each model drove. Regret is shown for three shortfall-pricing schemes -- top-up at the marginal unit, the peak unit, and unmet demand penalised at the dump price. \emph {Viol.} is physical-feasibility violations (velocity, differential pressure, unmet). The last two columns are the resulting dispatch: heat-pump energy share and running hours. The copperplate, blind to network loss, under-provisions the heat pump ($77.9$\,\%, 2063\,h) relative to the loss-aware node-resolved levels ($87.8$\,\%, $\sim $2310\,h), and pays the shortfall at the margin in winter hours it never planned for -- which is what the $+46$\,\% regret \emph {is}, physically}{table.caption.25}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces Estimation bias against decision regret for every level, as a percentage of the baseline operating cost. For the copperplate and the topology-only control the two carry \emph  {opposite signs}: a schedule that appears cheaper on paper is markedly more expensive to execute. Loss-aware levels show regret approximately equal to bias.}}{23}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:regret}{{7}{23}{Estimation bias against decision regret for every level, as a percentage of the baseline operating cost. For the copperplate and the topology-only control the two carry \emph {opposite signs}: a schedule that appears cheaper on paper is markedly more expensive to execute. Loss-aware levels show regret approximately equal to bias}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {8}{\ignorespaces Zone-boundary choice against the effects it must be compared with, on a logarithmic cost axis. With the total heat-loss capacity held equal across all twenty-four partitions, the residual routing effect (11\,EUR) is two orders of magnitude below the spatial-topology main effect (961\,EUR) and three below the loss main effect (19\,737\,EUR).}}{24}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
