\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Extended-physics effects, each isolated (R1.3) and reported against its method and a rigorous bound (R2.3). Trunk pressure and the linearisation error are \emph  {solved}: pressure to a $0.01$\,\% gap, and the PWL-vs-native temperature error by a fix-and-relax nonlinear re-solve (the MILP schedule is re-costed under native, non-linearised temperature propagation on a winter and an autumn week, both converged to $\le 0.03$\,\% gap). Node temperature propagation is additionally reported through the validated nonlinear forward reference -- \emph  {not} its solved MILP, whose free-variable temperature form is degenerate (the optimiser floors the supply temperature for $\sim $91\,\% of hours, collapsing losses), consistent with \S  2.4/\S  4.7. Transport delay reproduces $\ensuremath  {\mathrm  {L3}}$ \emph  {identically} -- to the last digit ($+0.00$\,\%): trunk travel times are sub-hourly, so on the hourly grid the delay is a zero-timestep shift that storage absorbs. Every effect is far below the loss main effect, and the linearisation error is small \emph  {and negative} -- the PWL model slightly over-states cost.}}{26}{table.caption.27}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:linearisation}{{9}{26}{Extended-physics effects, each isolated (R1.3) and reported against its method and a rigorous bound (R2.3). Trunk pressure and the linearisation error are \emph {solved}: pressure to a $0.01$\,\% gap, and the PWL-vs-native temperature error by a fix-and-relax nonlinear re-solve (the MILP schedule is re-costed under native, non-linearised temperature propagation on a winter and an autumn week, both converged to $\le 0.03$\,\% gap). Node temperature propagation is additionally reported through the validated nonlinear forward reference -- \emph {not} its solved MILP, whose free-variable temperature form is degenerate (the optimiser floors the supply temperature for $\sim $91\,\% of hours, collapsing losses), consistent with \S 2.4/\S 4.7. Transport delay reproduces $\Lthree $ \emph {identically} -- to the last digit ($+0.00$\,\%): trunk travel times are sub-hourly, so on the hourly grid the delay is a zero-timestep shift that storage absorbs. Every effect is far below the loss main effect, and the linearisation error is small \emph {and negative} -- the PWL model slightly over-states cost}{table.caption.27}{}}
