\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Fidelity design rule. The cost gap a copperplate misses (the loss burden) against the a-priori loss number $\lambda =\text  {annual loss}/\text  {annual demand}$. The parameter-free physics curve $b=\lambda /(1+\lambda )$ (navy) tracks the measured burden of the 135 synthetic networks (grey) and Memmingen (star) with $R^2=\textbf  {0.87}$. Shaded bands give the screening decision (copperplate/adder, calibrate an adder, or resolve nodes) read off from $\lambda $ alone, without solving.}}{28}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:rule}{{9}{28}{Fidelity design rule. The cost gap a copperplate misses (the loss burden) against the a-priori loss number $\lambda =\text {annual loss}/\text {annual demand}$. The parameter-free physics curve $b=\lambda /(1+\lambda )$ (navy) tracks the measured burden of the 135 synthetic networks (grey) and Memmingen (star) with $R^2=\textbf {0.87}$. Shaded bands give the screening decision (copperplate/adder, calibrate an adder, or resolve nodes) read off from $\lambda $ alone, without solving}{figure.9}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Drift of a frozen loss adder against trunk pipe length across the 135 synthetic networks. An adder calibrated on any one network mis-estimates the loss burden on others by a mean of 23.5 and up to 40.1 percentage points of cost, which is why the node-resolved model, computing the loss endogenously, is the transferable one.}}{29}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:drift}{{10}{29}{Drift of a frozen loss adder against trunk pipe length across the 135 synthetic networks. An adder calibrated on any one network mis-estimates the loss burden on others by a mean of 23.5 and up to 40.1 percentage points of cost, which is why the node-resolved model, computing the loss endogenously, is the transferable one}{figure.10}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Model size and solve time for the solved levels (single-year, hourly). Temperature propagation (L2) and the station levels (L4/L5) are forward-evaluated, not solved -- L2's free-variable temperature MILP is non-convex and degenerate. L3 adds the pressure PWL binaries.}}{29}{table.caption.29}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:computation}{{11}{29}{Model size and solve time for the solved levels (single-year, hourly). Temperature propagation (L2) and the station levels (L4/L5) are forward-evaluated, not solved -- L2's free-variable temperature MILP is non-convex and degenerate. L3 adds the pressure PWL binaries}{table.caption.29}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {11}{\ignorespaces Supply-temperature flexibility. Lowering the plant supply temperature reduces thermal loss but shrinks the temperature difference, so flow and pumping rise. The cost-optimal reduction is 17.5\,K; beyond 20\,K the pipe velocity limit binds, and hydraulics change from a negligible cost into the binding constraint.}}{30}{figure.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tsup}{{11}{30}{Supply-temperature flexibility. Lowering the plant supply temperature reduces thermal loss but shrinks the temperature difference, so flow and pumping rise. The cost-optimal reduction is 17.5\,K; beyond 20\,K the pipe velocity limit binds, and hydraulics change from a negligible cost into the binding constraint}{figure.11}{}}
